@HaonanFeng do you have any progress to share?

> That said, I don't think we do decision making with "reasons", only with "errors".

That's true.

> But we should fix how we compare errors. I think a proper comparison, at least for now would be:
> code is equal, reasons are equal, errors compare the same with `errors.Is`

I got your point. How about defining a generic `ComparerFn` in framework/interface.go (rough logic, can be tweaked):

```go
func ComparerFn(x, y *Status) bool {
	return x.code == y.code && cmp.Equal(x.reasons, y.reasons) && errors.Is(x.err, y.err)
}
```

Then in tests, call the `ComparerFn` to have a unified comparing behavior:

```go
if diff := cmp.Diff(gotStatus, tt.wantStatus, cmp.Comparer(framework.ComparerFn)); diff != "" {
	t.Errorf("Unexpected status (-want, +got): %s", diff)
}
```

Did you mean use `cmpopts.EquateErrors()` like this?

```
if diff:= cmp.Diff(gotStatus,tt.wantStatus,cmpopts.EquateErrors(),cmp.Exporter(func(r reflect.Type) bool {
	return true
})); diff != "" {
```

> Kind of. Export the Status fields (make the first letter uppercase) instead of using `cmp.Exporter`
Can we define a function `allowUnExportedType` export the "Status" fields .
```
func allowUnExportedType(t reflect.Type) bool{
	if t.Name()=="Status"{
		return true
	}
	return false
}
```
Then in `Diff`, `cmp.Exporter` judge whether it is "Status", if it is, then return the field of status:
`if diff:= cmp.Diff(gotStatus,tt.wantStatus,cmpopts.EquateErrors(),cmp.Exporter(allowUnExportedType)); diff != "" {`

We proposed to add an Equal function for Status struct in #98481 to easily compare two statuses with cmp. To be consistent with the behavior of `cmp.Equal(fmt.Errorf("err"), fmt.Errorf("err"), cmpopts.EquateErrors()) == false`, we decided to ignore comparing reasons of status.

```
func (s *Status) Equal(x *Status) bool {
	if s == nil || x == nil {
		return s.IsSuccess() && x.IsSuccess()
	}
	if s.code != x.code {
		return false
	}
	if s.code == Error {
		return cmp.Equal(s.err, x.err, cmpopts.EquateErrors())
	}
	return len(s.reasons) == 0 && len(x.reasons) == 0
}
```

However there are some test cases will compare other type status with reasons now. So the question here is do we really should care about the reasons behind the status in tests?

